Not much. =A0Vowel harmony would wreak havoc with the etymology of primitiv=
es, since vowels count as much as consonants in the word-building algorithm=
. =A0But, once that issue were solved, the harmony would be lost in derive =
predicates, since the vowels carry over to the components and changing them=
 to fit some preestablished pattern would interfere with their identificati=
on. =A0Finally, and perhaps most damning, vowel harmony does not affect fun=
ction words (mostly one syllable) where the problem of noisy channels is th=
e gravest, because of the packed word space and the relatively little use c=
ontext has in that area (many hard to distinguish words belong to categorie=
s that occur in the same frames). =A0So, vowel harmony does not help nearly=
 enough to be worth pursuing.


